## Characterization of pepsin-solubilised collagen recovered from mackerel
## (Scomber japonicus) bone and skin using subcritical water hydrolysis
\(^{a}\)Department of Food Science and Technology, Pukyong National University, 45 Yongso-ro, Nam-Gu, Busan 608-737, Republic of Korea
\(^{b}\)Department of Biochemistry and Molecular Biology, University of Rajshahi, Rajshahi-6205, 9 Bangladesh
\(^{c}\)Department of Fisheries and Marine Bioscience, Jashore University of Science and Technology, Jashore 7408, Bangladesh.
Abstract
This study was aimed at isolation of pepsin-solubilised collagen (PSC) from Mackerel (Scomber japonicus) bone and skin in order to effectively valorise these abundant wastes. The yield of PSC (8.10%) from skin was considerably higher than that from bone (1.75%). Based on the protein patterns, both PSCs were type I, and consisted of two \(\alpha\)-chains. Fourier-transform infrared spectra demonstrated that PSCs from the bone and skin exhibited a triple-helical structure. The denaturation temperatures (Td) of the PSCs from bone and skin were 27 and 30 \(^{\circ}\mathbf{C}\), respectively. Low-molecular-weight peptides (<1650 Da) were generated from both PSCs after subcritical water hydrolysis treatment. Glycine accounted for 30% of the total amino acids identified in both PSC hydrolysates. The antioxidant activities of both PSC hydrolysates were significantly higher than those of the isolated PSCs. Therefore, PSC hydrolysates can be used as a functional ingredient in the food, cosmetic and pharmaceutical industries.
Keywords: Antioxidant activity, Bone, Collagen, Mackerel, Skin, Subcritical water hydrolysis
## 1. Introduction
Mackerel (Scomber japonicus) is an important food source that is consumed worldwide. The Korean population consumes a large amount of mackerel daily as sashimi, or in fried and boiled foods. The production of these foods requires the removal of fish by-products, such as bone, skin and viscera. During this processing, a large amount of waste products is generated from the original raw materials [1] in fishmongers and processing factories. Improper disposal of these waste products might cause serious environmental pollution, due in part to the resulting offensive odour. However, fish waste is of high nutritional value, and so the proper utilisation of this waste can both prevent environment pollution and be commercially beneficial.
Collagen is a vertebrate structural protein which means it generally found in bone, skin and other connective tissues [2]. Traditionally, collagen has been isolated from the bones and skins of land-based animals such as cows, bovine and pigs. It is used widely in the food, cosmetic, biomedical and pharmaceutical industries due to its excellent biocompatibility, biodegradability and weak antigenicity [3]. In recent years, an increasing number of consumers have been concerned regarding the safety of collagen produced from land-based animals due to outbreaks of bovine spongiform encephalopathy (BSE), transmissible spongiform encephalopathy (TSE), foot-and-mouth disease (FMD) and avian influenza (AI) [4]. In addition, collagen obtained from pigs cannot be used as a component of certain foods for religious reasons. Therefore, there is a need to develop alternative collagen sources. Because collagen produced from fish waste, such as the bones and skin, is unlikely to be associated with BSE, TSE, FMD and AI, and is not subject to religious restrictions. Thus fish collagen has received increasing attention as an alternative source of collagen [5].
Obtaining effective functional materials requires the breakdown of macromolecules. Subcritical water hydrolysis (SWH) is a clean and rapid method of hydrolysing macromolecules that is an alternative to acid, base and enzymatic hydrolysis methods. The SWH involves the application of water at temperatures higher than its boiling point under high pressure to maintain its liquid. The temperature applied during hydrolysis process has an important impact on the hydrolysis efficiency and selectivity. The temperature above the normal boiling point of water is normally used in SWH. As a result, physical advantages including high diffusion, low viscosity, and low surface tension are achieved with this elevated temperature condition [6, 7]. It has the advantages of a shorter reaction time, no use of toxic solvents and the reduced formation of degradation products [8]. In contrast, acid- and alkali-based hydrolysis require extreme reaction conditions that can cause significant environmental pollution. Although enzymatic hydrolysis is specific for the macromolecule being broken down, it can involve long production cycles.
Modern-day consumers are increasingly aware of the benefits of nutritionally enhanced foods. Dietary supplements containing collagen hydrolysates are considered to have the potential to improve tendon or joint regeneration and reduce activity-related joint pain [9]. Collagen hydrolysates have the nutraceutical and/or functional properties that are necessary to create novel, innovative and healthy food products. Low-molecular-weight peptides are also easier for the body to absorb and utilise than whole proteins. In addition, high-molecular-weight collagen mixed with cosmetic products cannot penetrate deeply into human skin, and so production of low-molecular-weight collagen peptides (<3 kDa) is of great importance to the cosmetic industry. Collagen hydrolysates are also a good source of amino acids for individuals suffering from anorexia and anaemia, and for vegetarians. Glycine and proline, which perform an important function in the formation of connective tissue, are highly enriched in collagen
hydrolysates. In addition, it was reported recently that smaller peptides and amino acids in protein hydrolysates exhibit antioxidant activities [10].
Recently, collagens from several fish species have been isolated and characterised [3, 4, 11-13]. Interestingly, collagen produced from diverse fish species, habitats and tissues exhibited significantly different properties. Therefore, the aim of this study was to isolate collagen from the bones and skin of mackerel and characterise their potential in commercial applications to make more effective use of the waste generated during fish processing. The isolated collagens were hydrolysed using subcritical water treatment in order to produce lower molecular weight peptides. Moreover, the hydrolysed collagens were evaluated for their antioxidant activity using several assays.
## 2. Materials and methods
## 2.1. Materials
The mackerel (Scomber japonicus) samples were collected from F & F Co., Busan, Korea and transported to the laboratory in ice box. Pepsin, protein marker, standard collagen from calf skin and bovine achilles tendon, 2,2-diphenyl-1-picrylhydrazyl (DPPH), 2,2-azinobis(3-ethylbenzothiazoline-6-sulfonic acid) diammonium salt (ABTS), potassium ferricyanide, 3-(2-pyridyl)-5,6-bis-(4-phenyl-sulfonic acid)-1,2,4-triazine (ferrozine) and 6-hydroxy-2,5,7,8-tetramethylchromane-2-carboxylic acid (trolox) were purchased from Sigma-Aldrich (St. Louis, MO., USA). All other reagents and solvents used in this study were of analytical or HPLC grade.
## 2.2. Sample preparation
The bone and skin were separated manually from mackerel muscles, and the residue of any adhering tissues was removed. After washing thoroughly with running cold water, the
samples were lyophilised for \(\sim72\,\mathrm{h}\). The dried samples were then crushed in a mechanical blender and stored at \(-20\,^{\circ}\mathrm{C}\) until use.
## 2.3. Proximate analysis of mackerel bone and skin
The moisture, ash, lipid and protein contents of mackerel bone and skin were determined according to the AOAC method [14]. The non-protein content was calculated by subtracting the sum of the mass of moisture, ash, lipids and protein from the total mass.
## 2.4. Isolation of collagen from mackerel bone and skin
Collagen was isolated from mackerel bone and skin according to the methods described in [3, 15, 16] with slight modifications. All experimental procedures were performed at \(4\,^{\circ}\mathrm{C}\).
To remove non-collagenous substances, crushed bones were treated with 0.1 M NaOH at a sample/alkaline solution ratio of 1:10 (w/v). The mixture was stirred continuously for \(24\,\mathrm{h}\) using a magnetic stirrer at \(250\,\mathrm{rpm}\); the alkaline solution was changed every \(6\,\mathrm{h}\). Next, samples were washed with cold distilled water until the washing water reached a neutral \(\mathrm{pH}\), and then lyophilised (EYELA FDV-2100, Rikakikai Co. Ltd., Tokyo, Japan). Subsequently, the insoluble bone was decalcified with \(0.5\,\mathrm{M}\) ethylenediaminetetraacetic acid (EDTA, \(\mathrm{pH}\,7.5\)) at a sample/EDTA solution ratio of 1:10 (w/v) for 4 days; the EDTA solution was changed daily. The residue was then washed with cold distilled water, and fat was removed with \(10\%\) (v/v) butyl alcohol at a sample/solvent ratio of 1:10 (w/v) for \(24\,\mathrm{h}\). The residue was then washed again using cold distilled water. Subsequently, the remaining residue was extracted using \(0.57\,\mathrm{M}\) acetic acid containing \(0.1\%\) (w/v) pepsin for 3 days at a sample/solution ratio of 1:10 (w/v) followed by centrifugation at \(13700\times\mathrm{g}\) for \(50\,\mathrm{min}\). The residue was then re-extracted with the same solution for 3 days, and centrifuged under identical conditions. Each viscous solution was mixed and salting out by adding NaCl to a final concentration of \(2.0\,\mathrm{M}\). The solutions were incubated for 24
h, and precipitation was assessed. The precipitates were harvested by centrifugation at \(13700 \times g\) for \(30\;\mathrm{min}\), and then dissolved in \(0.57\;\mathrm{M}\) acetic acid. The solutions were dialysed against \(0.1\;\mathrm{M}\) acetic acid and distilled water in a dialysis bag for 2 days, and then lyophilised.
The crushed mackerel skin was treated with \(0.1\;\mathrm{M}\) NaOH for \(24\;\mathrm{h}\) at a sample/alkaline solution ratio of \(1:35\;(\mathrm{w/v})\), and mixed using a magnetic stirrer. The solution was changed four times in 1 day, and then lyophilised after washing with cold distilled water. Next, the lyophilised samples were de-fatted with \(10\%\) (w/v) butyl alcohol for \(24\;\mathrm{h}\) at the above ratio. The de-fatted residues were washed with cold distilled water, and then mixed with \(0.57\;\mathrm{M}\) acetic acid containing \(0.1\%\) (w/v) pepsin for 3 days at a sample/solution ratio of \(1:35\;(\mathrm{w/v})\). Further procedures were carried out according to those described above for collagen isolation from mackerel bone. Proximate analysis was done after PSCs isolation from mackerel bone and skin.
## 2.5. Characterisation of PSCs
## 2.5.1. Sodium dodecyl sulphate polyacrylamide gel electrophoresis (SDS-PAGE)
Proteins were analysed by SDS-PAGE according to the method of [17] using a \(3.0\%\) stacking gel and a \(5.0\%\) resolving gel. PSC samples \((2\;\mathrm{mg})\) were dissolved in \(1.0\;\mathrm{mL}\) of \(0.02\;\mathrm{M}\) sodium phosphate buffer \((\mathrm{pH}\,7.2)\), and then mixed with sample buffer (1 M Tris-HCl, \(\mathrm{pH}\,6.8\), containing \(10\%\) SDS, \(25\%\) glycerol, \(2\%\) bromophenol blue and \(5\%\) 2-mercaptoethanol) at a \(1:1\) ratio (v/v). Then, \(20\;\upmu\mathrm{L}\) of each sample \((20\;\upmu\mathrm{g}\) protein) were loaded onto the polyacrylamide gels, and separated by electrophoresis at a constant current of \(30\;\mathrm{mA}\). Collagens from calf skin and bovine achilles tendons were isolated according to the above procedures and used as standards for comparison with the collagen harvested from mackerel bone and skin. After electrophoresis, the gels were stained using \(0.1\%\) (w/v) Coomassie Brilliant Blue R-250 for \(1\;\mathrm{h}\), and then destained using \(7.5\%\) (v/v) methanol and \(10\%\) (v/v) glacial acetic acid until clear
protein bands were visualised. High-molecular-weight (MW) markers were used to estimate the MW of proteins.
## 2.5.2. Fourier-transform infrared (FT-IR) spectra analysis
The lyophilised PSCs from mackerel bone and skin FT-IR spectra were obtained using a Perkin Elmer (USA), Spectrum X. Spectra were measured over the frequency range 4000–650 \(\mathrm{cm}^{-1}\), with a resolution of \(4\;\mathrm{cm}^{-1}\).
## 2.5.3. Measurement of viscosity
A Brookfield DVII + Pro viscometer (Brookfield Engineering Laboratories, Inc., Middleboro, MA 02346 USA) was used to measure the viscosity of PSCs from mackerel bone and skin according to the method described by [16], with slight modifications. \(8\;\mathrm{mL}\) of \(0.1\%\) (w/v) PSC in \(0.1\;\mathrm{M}\) acetic acid were incubated at \(10\,^{\circ}\mathrm{C}\) for \(20\;\mathrm{min}\), and then placed in a vessel. Spindle SC4-18 with agitation at \(150\;\mathrm{rpm}\) was then used to measure viscosity, which was expressed as centipose (cP).
## 2.5.4. Denaturation temperature (Td) determination
The denaturation temperature (Td) was measured according to the method of [18] with a modification. \(8\;\mathrm{mL}\) of \(0.1\%\) (w/v) PSC in \(0.1\;\mathrm{M}\) acetic acid were heated from \(10{-}40^{\circ}\mathrm{C}\) at intervals of \(3^{\circ}\mathrm{C}\). The solution was held at the designated temperature for \(20\;\mathrm{min}\) prior to the measurement of viscosity. The thermal denaturation curve of the PSC was determined by plotting the fractional change in viscosity against temperature. Fractional viscosity was calculated for each temperature as follows: fractional viscosity = (measured viscosity/maximum
viscosity). The Td was determined to be the temperature at which fractional viscosity was close to 0.5.
## 2.5.5. Collagen hydrolysate production using subcritical water hydrolysis (SWH)
Small-scale SWH apparatus was used for PSC hydrolysate production. SWH was performed in a \(200\;\mathrm{mL}\) batch reactor made from Hastelloy 276 with a temperature controller. PSCs and water at the ratio of 1:200 (w/v) were placed in the reactor, and the lid was closed. Then the reactor was purged three times with nitrogen gas to remove any oxygen gas from it. The reactor was heated using an electric heater at the desired temperature \((200{-}250\ ^{\circ}\mathrm{C})\) and pressure (30–70 bar). The required to reach the desired temperature was 36 to 54 min and the holding time at that temperature was 3 min. The temperature and pressure in the reactor were measured during each experiment using the temperature controller and pressure gauge, respectively. The samples were stirred using a magnetic stirrer at \(150\;\mathrm{rpm}\). After that, reactor was cooled by connecting a cooling circulatory water bath. Hydrolysate samples were collected from the reactor, and then filtered using a filter paper.
## 2.5.6. Peptide molecular weight analysis
\(1~\upmu\mathrm{L}\) of PSC hydrolysate samples from mackerel bone and skin were spotted onto a polished steel 384 target plate, mixed with \(1~\upmu\mathrm{L}\) of 2,5-dihydroxybenzoic acid (DHB) matrix solution containing \(1\%\) THF and dried. Each sample was analysed using matrix-assisted laser desorption ionisation time-of-flight mass spectrum (MALDI-TOF MS) on an Ultraflex III mass spectrometer (Bruker Daltonics, Germany) equipped with a \(337\ \mathrm{nm}\) pulse nitrogen laser.
Measurements in the \(\mathbf{m}/\mathbf{z}\) range of 700–6000 were acquired in the positive ionisation and reflection modes by accumulating data from 200 laser shots.
## 2.5.7. Analysis of amino acid composition
PSC hydrolysates from mackerel bone and skin were filtered and loaded onto a S430 (SYKAM) amino acid auto-analyser for analysis of the amino acid content. The cation separation column LCA K07/Li \((4.6 \times 150\,\mathrm{mm})\) was used with a column temperature of \(37{-}74\,^{\circ}\mathrm{C}\) and a buffer pH range of 2.90–7.95. The mobile phase was \(5\,\mathrm{mM}\) \(p\)-toluenesulphonic acid solution at a flow rate of \(0.45\,\mathrm{mL/min}\). A mixture of \(5\,\mathrm{mM}\) \(p\)-toluenesulphonic acid, \(20\,\mathrm{mM}\) of Bis-Tris and \(100\,\mathrm{mM}\) of EDTA was used as the post-column reagent at a flow rate of \(0.25\,\mathrm{mL/min}\). The excitation and emission wavelengths were maintained at 440 and \(570\,\mathrm{nm}\) for operational conditions.
## 2.5.8. Antioxidant activities
## 2.5.8.1 DPPH free radical scavenging assay
The free radicals scavenging effects of PSC hydrolysates from mackerel bone and skin were determined by the method of Yen and Chen [19] with slight modifications. Briefly, 3.95 mL of \(0.1\,\mathrm{mM}\) DPPH solution in methanol were added to test tubes containing \(0.1\,\mathrm{mL}\) of PSC samples at various concentrations before and after hydrolysis. The mixtures were vortexed for 10 s, and then incubated at room temperature for \(30\,\mathrm{min}\) in the dark. The absorbance at \(517\,\mathrm{nm}\) of all samples was measured. The percentage DPPH free radical scavenging activity was calculated using the following equation: \((1 - [A_s/A_c]) \times 100\%\), where, \(A_s =\) absorbance of the sample and control at \(517\,\mathrm{nm}\) and \(A_c =\) absorbance of the blank at \(517\,\mathrm{nm}\). The sample blank and control (various concentrations of trolox in methanol) were analysed as described above.
## 2.5.8.2. ABTS\(^+\) free radical scavenging assay
ABTS\(^+\) free radical scavenging assays were performed as described by [20] with slight modification. ABTS was dissolved in water to a concentration of \(7\ \mathrm{mM/L}\). ABTS\(^+\) was produced by reacting an equal volume of ABTS stock solution with \(2.45\ \mathrm{mM/L}\) potassium persulphate and incubating the mixture in the dark at room temperature for \(16\ \mathrm{h}\) before use. To assay samples, the ABTS\(^+\) stock solution was diluted in \(80\%\) methanol to an absorbance of \(0.70 \pm 0.02\) at \(734\ \mathrm{nm}\). After the addition of \(3.95\ \mathrm{mL}\) of diluted ABTS\(^+\) to \(0.05\ \mathrm{mL}\) of PSC samples (at different concentrations before and after hydrolysis), mixtures were kept in a dark environment at room temperature for \(6\ \mathrm{min}\). The absorbance at \(734\ \mathrm{nm}\) of all samples was measured. The percentage ABTS\(^+\) free radical scavenging activity was calculated using the following formula: \((1 - [As/Ac]) \times 100\%\), where, \(As =\) absorbance of the sample and control at \(734\ \mathrm{nm}\) and \(Ac =\) absorbance of the blank at \(734\ \mathrm{nm}\). The sample blank and control (various concentrations of trolox in \(80\%\) methanol) were analysed as described above.
## 2.5.8.3. Ferric reducing power assay
The \(\mathrm{Fe}^{3+}\) reducing power of PSC samples was determined following the method of [21] with a slight modification. PSC samples \((0.125\ \mathrm{mL})\) at various concentrations before and after hydrolysis were mixed with \(0.625\ \mathrm{mL}\) of \(0.2\ \mathrm{M}\) phosphate buffer \((\mathrm{pH}\ 6.6)\) and \(0.625\ \mathrm{mL}\) of \(1\%\) potassium ferricyanide. The mixtures were incubated at \(50^{\circ}\mathrm{C}\) for \(20\ \mathrm{min}\), and \(0.625\ \mathrm{mL}\) of \(10\%\) trichloroacetic acid was then added followed by centrifugation at \(855 \times \mathrm{g}\) for \(10\ \mathrm{min}\). The upper layer of solution \((1.0\ \mathrm{mL})\) was mixed with \(1.0\ \mathrm{mL}\) of distilled water and \(1.0\ \mathrm{mL}\) of \(0.1\%\) ferric chloride, and the absorbance at \(700\ \mathrm{nm}\) was read. A rise in absorbance reflected an increasing reducing power.
## 2.5.8.4. \(\mathbf{Fe}^{2+}\) chelating assay
The ability of samples to chelate \(\mathrm{Fe}^{2+}\) was determined using the method of Decker & Welch [22] with modifications. PSC samples (0.1 mL) at various concentrations before and after hydrolysis were mixed with 3.0 mL of distilled water, followed by incubation with 50 \(\upmu\)L of 2 mM FeCl\(_2\) and 0.1 mL of 5 mM ferrozine for 20 min at room temperature. The absorbance at 562 nm was then read. Various concentrations of EDTA were used as control samples; distilled water was used as the negative control. Chelating activity (\%) was calculated as follows:
Chelating activity \((\%)=(1-\frac{A_{562\mathrm{~of~sample}}}{A_{562\mathrm{~of~control}}})\times100\)
## 2.6. Statistical analysis
All experiments were performed in triplicate. The data were subjected to analysis of variance (ANOVA), and the differences between means were evaluated using Duncan's multiple range test. The SPSS statistics software (SPSS version 15.0 for windows, SPSS Inc., Chicago, IL, USA) was used for data analysis.
## 3. Results and discussion
## 3.1. Proximate composition of mackerel bone, skin and PSCs
Protein was the major component of freeze-dried mackerel bone and skin, accounting for \(47.25\pm1.25\) and \(49.49\pm1.52\%\) of the content, respectively; they also contained moisture (6.68 \(\pm\,0.07\) and \(4.60\pm0.06\%\) ), ash \((6.89\pm0.06\) and \(4.50\pm0.05\%\) ), lipids \((17.59\pm1.10\) and \(29.47\pm\) \(1.26\%\) ) and non-protein substances \((21.59\pm1.10\) and \(11.94\pm0.76\%\) ). When mackerel bone and skin were compared directly, the protein and lipid content was highest in the skin, whereas the moisture, ash and non-protein contents were higher in bone. The PSC yields from mackerel bone and skin were \(1.75\pm0.07\) and \(8.10\pm0.12\%\) , respectively. Generally, pepsin can cleave
intermolecular cross-links at the telopeptide region, leading to increased solubility [2]. Therefore, we used pepsin during collagen isolation. The yield of collagen (wet-weight) from fish bone was \(1.6\%\) from bigeye snapper and \(1.3\%\) from bighead carp [1, 3]. Conversely, the wet-weight yield from fish skin was \(7.6\%\) from unicorn leatherjacket, \(7.1\%\) from bigeye snapper (Priacanthus macracanthus) and \(7.7\%\) from striped catfish [23-25]. The differences in PSC yield could be attributed to the differences in fish species, biological conditions and preparative methods. These observations are consistent with Liu et al., [3]. The protein content of PSCs isolated from mackerel bone and skin was comparable, at \(90.05 \pm 2.34\) and \(86.89 \pm 2.48\%\), respectively. In addition, PSCs from mackerel bone and skin contained similar moisture (\(6.28 \pm 0.12\) and \(7.48 \pm 0.10\%\)) and ash (\(3.48 \pm 0.09\) and \(5.37 \pm 0.06\%\)) contents, and a lower fat content (\(0.19 \pm 0.02\) and \(0.26 \pm 0.03\%\), respectively). Similar results were reported for collagen isolated from blacktip shark [26].
## 3.2. Analysis of PSCs by SDS-PAGE
The subunit composition of PSCs from mackerel bone and skin are shown in Fig. 1. Both PSC contained mainly two \(\alpha\)-chains (\(\alpha_{1}\) and \(\alpha_{2}\)), similar to many other fish species [3, 11, 27]. The approximate MWs of both PSCs were identical: \(126\,\mathrm{kDa}\) for \(\alpha_{1}\) and \(116\,\mathrm{kDa}\) for \(\alpha_{2}\), similar to the standard collagen isolated from catfish and bovine achilles tendons. Electrophoresis mobility and subunit composition analyses suggested that the PSCs from mackerel bone and skin were composed mainly of type-I collagen, a heterotrimer containing two identical \(\alpha_{1}\)-chains and one \(\alpha_{2}\)-chain in the molecular form of \([\alpha_{1}(\mathrm{I})]_{2}\alpha_{2}(\mathrm{I})\). This is consistent with previous reports of skin collagen from deep-sea redfish [27], skin, scale and bone collagen from carp [28] and fins, scales, skins, bones and swim bladders collagen from bighead carp [3]. In
contrast, [29] reported that collagen from the skin and muscles of rainbow trout contained three non-identical \(\alpha\)-chains, \(\alpha_{1}(\mathrm{I})\alpha_{2}(\mathrm{I})\alpha_{3}(\mathrm{I})\), whereas Zhang et al., [11] reported that collagen from jellyfish consisted mainly of homotrimeric \(\alpha\)-chains, \([\alpha_{1}(\mathrm{I})]_{3}\). The molecular form of collagen thus likely differs among species. Moreover, PSCs from mackerel bone and skin contained one \(\beta\)-chain with a MW of \(\sim205\;\mathrm{kDa}\).
## 3.3. FT-IR spectra of PSC from mackerel bone and skin
The FT-IR spectra of PSCs from mackerel bone and skin are shown in Fig. 2. The amide A bands of PSCs from mackerel bone and skin were detected at 3283 and \(3285\;\mathrm{cm}^{-1}\), respectively, suggesting the presence of N-H stretching vibration and hydrogen bonds. Doyle, Bendit, & Blout [30] reported that a free N-H stretching vibration occurs commonly in the range \(3400{-}3440\;\mathrm{cm}^{-1}\), and that this position is shifted to a lower wavenumber when the NH group of a peptide forms a hydrogen bond. The amide B band of both PSC collagens was observed at 2922 \(\mathrm{cm}^{-1}\), which is related to the asymmetrical stretch of \(\mathrm{CH}_{2}\) [18, 31].
Heu et al., [32] reported that the wavenumbers of the amide I, amide II and amide III bands were associated directly with the configuration of the collagen. The amide I band (1600–\(1660\;\mathrm{cm}^{-1}\)) is associated with the stretching vibrations of carbonyl groups \(\scriptstyle(\mathbf{C}=\mathbf{O})\) in peptides, and is the most important factor when investigating the secondary structure of a polypeptide [31]. Amide II \((\sim\!1550\;\mathrm{cm}^{-1})\) is associated with N-H bending and C-N stretching vibrations, and a shift to lower wavenumbers suggests the existence of hydrogen bonds [23]. Amide III (1220–1320 \(\mathrm{cm}^{-1}\)) is related to C-N stretching vibration and N-H deformation, and an absorption ratio of 1 between the amide III and \(1453{-}1455\;\mathrm{cm}^{-1}\) bands indicates an intact triple helical structure [23]. PSCs from mackerel bone and skin exhibited amide bands at wavenumbers of 1650 and 1651
\(\mathrm{cm}^{-1}\) for amide I, 1537 and \(1548~\mathrm{cm}^{-1}\) for amide II and 1237 and \(1238~\mathrm{cm}^{-1}\) for amide III, respectively. Ratios of 0.98 and 0.99 were obtained for the PSCs from mackerel bone and skin, respectively, suggesting that the triple-helical structure was not affected by pepsin digestion during collagen isolation.
## 3.4. PSC viscosity and its thermal behaviour
High viscosity is an important characteristic of collagen. The viscosities of PSCs from mackerel bone and skin were \(18.34 \pm 0.25\) and \(20.26 \pm 0.21~\mathrm{cP}\), respectively. However, the viscosity of PSC from mackerel skin was slightly higher than that from bone, possibly due to the high proportion of collagen polymers, which results in a higher mean molecular weight.
Fig. 3 shows the transition curve of the denaturation temperatures (Td) of PSCs from mackerel bone and skin. The Td of the PSCs was determined from the viscosity changes upon heating. The viscosity of both PSCs decreased as temperature increased from \(10{-}40~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}\). The Tds of PSCs from mackerel bone and skin were 27 and \(30~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}\), respectively. This is higher than those reported for arabesque greenling \((15.4~^{\circ}\mathrm{C})\) and deep-sea redfish \((16.1~^{\circ}\mathrm{C})\) [27, 33], but lower than those for bigeye snapper (P. tayenus; \(31.3~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}\)), blacktip shark \((34.37~^{\circ}\mathrm{C})\) and striped catfish \((39.6~^{\circ}\mathrm{C})\) [1, 25, 26]. The Td values of collagen from various sources correlated with the imino acid content, the temperature of the normal habitat, the season, and age [15, 25].
## 3.5. Hydrolysis PSC yield
PSCs from mackerel bone and skin were hydrolysed completely at temperatures of 200–250 \({}^{\circ}\mathrm{C}\) and pressures of 30–70 bar.
3.6. Peptide MW and the predicted peptide sequence of PSC hydrolysates
The molecular weight and peptide content of each PSC from the bone and skin of mackerel at temperatures of \(200{-}250\;^{\circ}\mathrm{C}\) and pressures of 30–70 bar, assessed using MALDI-TOF mass spectra data, are shown in Table 1. A MW range of 759.12–988.20 Da was obtained from the PSC hydrolysate from mackerel bone at a temperature \(200\,^{\circ}\mathrm{C}\) and pressure of 30 bar. The peptide intensity (3335.74) was particularly high at a MW of 984.21 Da. No peptide peak was identified in mackerel bone PSC hydrolysates treated at \(250\;^{\circ}\mathrm{C}\) and 70 bar within the selected MALDI-TOF MS \(\mathbf{m}/\mathbf{z}\) data range (700–6000 Da). Treatment of PSC hydrolysates at \(250\;^{\circ}\mathrm{C}\) and 70 bar likely results in generation of lower-MW peptides \((<\!700\,\mathrm{Da})\) and an increased amount of organic compounds. Nine predicted peptides were identified from PSC hydrolysates at \(200\;^{\circ}\mathrm{C}\) and 30 bar; these had the following amino acid sequences: K.TKATLAR.M, K.RMDLAR.I, K.VTFNRK.Q, K.ATLARMAR.G, R.MARGAMVR.F, R.FVFIIYQH.-, - .MKIIIAPAK.K, M.ADAELEAIR.Q and K.TLWHCSDK.L, as determined using the Mascot database.
PSC hydrolysates from mackerel skin obtained using temperatures of \(200{-}250\;^{\circ}\mathrm{C}\) and pressures of 30–70 bar yielded MW ranges of 789.36–1632.92 and 952.30–1368.13 Da. The highest peptide intensities were 2173.88 for 1216.11 Da at \(200\;^{\circ}\mathrm{C}\) and 30 bar, and 5328.47 for 952.30 Da at \(250\;^{\circ}\mathrm{C}\) and 70 bar. Smaller-MW products with a higher intensity were produced due to the application of high temperature and pressure. The amino acid sequences of the predicted peptides from mackerel skin PSC hydrolysates at \(200\;^{\circ}\mathrm{C}\) and 30 bar were R.SDGSRIR.F, -MIQMQTKLK.-, K.DAAADKAEDVK.D, K.EGLGKLTGNEK.L, R.RYANIGDVIK.Y, K.QLDTLGNDKGR.L, K.DAVEDKVEDAK.E, K.KGDVYDAVVVR.T and R.SAQFMKIVSLAPEVL.-, compared with R.HAEVVASIK.A, R.SVDPGSPAAR.S, - .MKTAQELR.V and K.NLLTGSASESVYK.A at \(250\;^{\circ}\mathrm{C}\) and 70 bar. Based on the peptide MW,
the amino acid sequences and composition of PSC hydrolysates from mackerel skin and bone differed according to the temperature and pressure applied. Kittiphattanabawon et al., [26] and Liu et al., [3] reported previously that the MW of collagen was reduced to maximums of 48.8 and \(20\,\mathrm{kDa}\) using V8-protease from blacktip shark and bighead carp; these values were greater than those of PSC hydrolysates from mackerel bone and skin. This suggests that SWH enables the production of lower-MW peptides.
## 3.7. Amino acid composition of PSC hydrolysates
The amino acid composition of collagen from mackerel bone and skin, expressed as grams of amino acid per \(1{,}000\mathrm{g}\) collagen, is shown in Table 2. The concentrations of amino acids differed mackerel bone and skin. In addition, the temperature and pressure used affected the concentration of amino acids recovered from mackerel bone. Among the amino acids identified in both PSC hydrolysates at \(200{-}250\ ^{\circ}\mathrm{C}\) and 30–70 bar, glycine was the major component, forming \(27.90{-}29.44\%\) of the recovered amino acids from mackerel bone PSCs and 32.38– \(35.25\%\) of those from mackerel skin. The glycine content of recovered amino acids was \(30.8\%\) from jellyfish [11], \(35.7\%\) from deep-sea redfish [27], \(33.2–34.1\%\) from bighead carp [3] and \(32.30\%\) from blacktip shark [26], which are comparable with the values reported here for mackerel bone and skin.
The total imino acid (proline and hydroxyproline) contents of both PSC hydrolysates from mackerel bone and skin at \(200{-}250\ ^{\circ}\mathrm{C}\) and 30–70 bar were \(13.11{-}14.07\%\) and 17.41– \(17.46\%\) , respectively, which are comparable with those reported for other cold water fish species \((16{-}18\%)\) [4, 31, 34] reported that a lower imino acid content and denaturation temperature
correlated directly with the thermal stability of proteins. Therefore, the thermal stability of PSC from mackerel skin was likely higher than those from bone.
Mackerel bone and skin PSC hydrolysates at \(200\,^{\circ}\mathrm{C}\) and 30 bar were both rich in alanine, valine, phenylalanine, leucine, isoleucine, tyrosine and histidine. When temperature and pressure were increased, the content of most recovered amino acids decreased slightly. Cheng et al., [35] reported that the maximum yield of most amino acids was achieved at temperatures of \(180{-}220\,^{\circ}\mathrm{C}\). Arginine and serine were not detected in both PSC hydrolysate at \(250\,^{\circ}\mathrm{C}\) and 70 bar; this is consistent with both the thermal instability of several amino acids and the increased production of organic compounds in both hydrolysates at elevated temperatures. Abdelmoez, Nakahasi, & Yoshida [36] reported that arginine was sensitive to temperatures of \(\sim230\,^{\circ}\mathrm{C}\) during SWH. Most essential amino acids were detected in PSC hydrolysates from mackerel bone and skin, the exceptions being threonine and tryptophan. Other studies reported that cysteine and tryptophan were not detected in fish collagen hydrolysates [11, 27, 31]. These differences might be due to use of diverse fish species and various hydrolysate production processes.
## 3.8. Antioxidant activities of PSC hydrolysates
## 3.8.1. DPPH free radical scavenging activity
In the present study, PSC hydrolysates from mackerel bone and skin isolated at 200–250 \(^{\circ}\mathrm{C}\) and 30–70 bar were effective scavengers of DPPH radicals, as shown in Fig.4a. In contrast, no DPPH free radical scavenging activity was found in either PSC. Increasing concentrations of both PSC hydrolysates exhibited elevated DPPH free radical scavenging activity. When DPPH encounters a hydrogen donating substance, the radical is scavenged, resulting in a reduction in absorbance [37]. During hydrolysis of both PSCs, a wide range of smaller peptides and amino
acids with the capacity to function as hydrogen donors were generated; these could then react with DPPH free radicals, convert them into more stable products and terminate the radical chain reaction. The \(\mathbf{IC}_{50}\) values of PSC hydrolysates at \(200{-}250\ ^{\circ}\mathrm{C}\) and 30–70 bar were \(8.71\pm0.14\) and \(8.38\pm0.13~\mathrm{mg/mL}\) from mackerel bone, and \(9.57\pm0.12\) and \(7.58\pm0.10\;\mathrm{mg/mL}\) from mackerel skin, compared with \(0.35\pm0.05~\mathrm{mg/mL}\) for trolox. The PSC hydrolysate produced from mackerel skin at \(250\;^{\circ}\mathrm{C}\) and 70 bar showed a reduced \(\mathbf{IC}_{50}\) value compared with the hydrolysate from mackerel bone.
## 3.8.2. ABTS free radical scavenging activity
An ABTS free radical cation is relatively stable, and its green colour is reduced by the presence of antioxidant compounds with electron-donating capacity [38]. The ABTS free radical scavenging activities of various PSC concentrations before and after hydrolysis are shown in Fig. 4b. The ABTS scavenging activities of both PSC hydrolysates were significantly higher than those of the isolated PSCs. This suggests that the antioxidant compounds were most likely hydrophilic [38]. The \(\mathbf{IC}_{50}\) values of PSC from mackerel bone were \(10.77\pm0.11~\mathrm{mg/mL}\) (isolated), \(4.18\pm0.07~\mathrm{mg/mL}\) (hydrolysate at \(200\;^{\circ}\mathrm{C}\) and 30 bar) and \(2.61\pm0.05~\mathrm{mg/mL}\) (hydrolysate at \(250\;^{\circ}\mathrm{C}\) and 70 bar). The corresponding values from mackerel skin were \(12.12\pm0.13~\mathrm{mg/mL}\) (isolated), \(4.05\pm0.08~\mathrm{mg/mL}\) (hydrolysate at \(200\;^{\circ}\mathrm{C}\) and 30 bar) and \(2.50\pm0.05~\mathrm{mg/mL}\) (hydrolysate at \(250\;^{\circ}\mathrm{C}\) and 70 bar). In contrast, the \(\mathbf{IC}_{50}\) value of standard trolox was 0.25 \(\mathrm{mg/mL}\), which was lower than both PSCs before and after hydrolysis. Antioxidant activity is influenced by changes in the size, quantity and composition of free amino acids and smaller peptides [19].
## 3.8.3. Ferric reducing power activity
As shown in Fig. 4c, \(\mathrm{Fe}^{3+}\) was transformed to \(\mathrm{Fe}^{2+}\) in the presence of PSCs both before and after hydrolysis. Various concentrations of PSC hydrolysates from mackerel skin obtained at \(250\;^{\circ}\mathrm{C}\) and 70 bar exhibited greater ferric-reducing activity compared with those from bone. This suggests that PSC hydrolysates function by donating electrons to free radicals, consistent with [39].
## 3.8.4. \(\mathbf{Fe}^{2+}\) chelating activity
The \(\mathrm{Fe}^{2+}\) chelating activities of various concentrations of mackerel bone and skin PSC hydrolysates at \(200{-}250\;^{\circ}\mathrm{C}\) and 30–70 bar are shown in Fig. 4d. The isolated PSCs did not exhibit \(\mathrm{Fe}^{2+}\) chelating activity. In contrast, both PSC hydrolysates exerted similar \(\mathrm{Fe}^{2+}\) chelating effects. However, the \(\mathrm{Fe}^{2+}\) chelating activities of both PSC hydrolysates were increased at elevated temperatures and pressures. The PSC hydrolysates might have contained diverse peptides of varying sizes and amino acid sequences, with different modes of action. This observation is consistent with [38]. Therefore, PSC hydrolysates could function as \(\mathrm{Fe}^{2+}\) chelators. The \(\mathrm{IC}_{50}\) values of PSCs from mackerel bone were \(8.21\pm0.15\;\mathrm{mg/mL}\) (hydrolysate at \(200\;^{\circ}\mathrm{C}\) and 30 bar) and \(7.27\pm0.14\;\mathrm{mg/mL}\) (hydrolysate at \(250\;^{\circ}\mathrm{C}\) and 70 bar), compared with \(7.91\pm0.11\;\mathrm{mg/mL}\) (hydrolysate at \(200\;^{\circ}\mathrm{C}\) and 30 bar) and \(7.01\pm0.12\;\mathrm{mg/mL}\) (hydrolysate at \(250\;^{\circ}\mathrm{C}\) and 70 bar) from mackerel skin. The \(\mathrm{IC}_{50}\) value of EDTA was \(0.34\pm0.06\;\mathrm{mg/mL}\), lower than those of the PSC hydrolysates.
## 4. Conclusions
PSCs were isolated successfully from bone and skin of mackerel; the yield from skin was considerably higher. Both PSCs were most likely type I collagen, and maintained their triple helical structure. The thermal stability of PSC from skin was slightly higher than that from bone. Diverse small, MW-containing peptides were produced from both PSCs after SWH treatment;
these exhibited multifunctional antioxidant activities greater than that of the isolated PSC. Therefore, PSC hydrolysates from mackerel bone and skin have the potential to serve as an alternative source of isolated collagen.
